      "text": "**One number, enormous, plus a large face.** This is the part that is easy to get wrong, so it is stated first. In every working reference the key figure is one of the largest objects in the frame, often a third of its width, and the face is big and well lit. None of them show a table, a list, or a full dashboard at its native scale.\n\nThe face sits in a corner as a webcam-style overlay, but large enough to read as a person rather than a stamp. Sometimes a red circle or arrow around the number. Text ranges from a short headline down to none at all, since the number is already carrying the message.\n\nThe discipline that separates the good ones from a raw screenshot: browser chrome, sidebars, taskbars and desktop clutter are cropped out or covered. Only the interface that matters survives.\n\nUse for anything where the proof is on a screen. This is the pattern for trade recaps, trade breakdowns, dashboards, and payout ledgers.\n\n**On showing the number.** A real figure on a real interface is blunt transparency and it is catchy, so show it. The ledger, the P&L, the payout column with its account IDs and dates: that is the asset, not a liability. What makes a number obnoxious is coupling it with watches, cars or lifestyle, at which point it stops being evidence and becomes a status claim. The variable is the coupling, never the figure. Full rule in packaging rules rule 5."
